          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "A three-character category is usually **not** a complete code. Most ICD-10-CM categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character before they are billable. I10 (essential hypertension) is the exception: it is complete at three characters and takes no further characters. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\nThe main convention is that the three-character form is the category, and the extra characters add detail such as cause, site, severity or laterality. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.) Examples from the retrieved material:\n\n- **Asthma (J45)** is never used at three characters. Its remaining characters combine a severity axis with a状态 axis for the current encounter. (Source: *Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*.)\n- **COPD (J44)** is never billed at three characters. The fourth character records whether an infection or acute exacerbation is present. The unspecified code is used for a stable presentation. (Source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*.)\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11)** is never used at three characters. Its fourth and later characters name the complication. Diabetes with no complications has its own code ending in 9, such as E11.9. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not give a step-by-step test for every category. They show the pattern and the examples above, but they do not list a general procedure for checking any three-character code. To confirm whether a specific category has subdivisions, check the official ICD-10-CM tabular list, or ask a certified medical coder or your clinician's billing team.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
